nk area) extends to the orange and faintly green line of the lowest airglow, at about one hundred kilometres at the edge of space and the lower edge of the thermosphere (invisible). Continuing with green and red bands of auroras stretching over several hundred kilometres.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAntarctic_aurora_ESA313457.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Aurora_shapes.jpg/500px-Aurora_shapes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Aurora_shapes.jpg/1280px-Aurora_shapes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Aurora_shapes.jpg/500px-Aurora_shapes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Different forms","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAurora_shapes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Magenta_G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_11.jpg/500px-Magenta_G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Magenta_G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_11.jpg/1280px-Magenta_G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Magenta_G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_11.jpg/500px-Magenta_G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Divergence point of a coronal aurora","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMagenta_G5_aurora_over_Tuntorp%2C_Lysekil_Municipality_11.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/AuroraBorealisOkeford20240510-01.jpg/500px-AuroraBorealisOkeford20240510-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/AuroraBorealisOkeford20240510-01.jpg/1280px-AuroraBorealisOkeford20240510-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/AuroraBorealisOkeford20240510-01.jpg/500px-AuroraBorealisOkeford20240510-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"2024 appearance seen in England radiating blue through red aurora","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAuroraBorealisOkeford20240510-01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Keogram_explainer.gif/330px-Keogram_explainer.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Keogram_explainer.gif/330px-Keogram_explainer.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Construction of a keogram from one night's recording by an all-sky camera, 6/7 September 2021. Keograms are commonly used to visualize changes in auroras over time.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKeogram_explainer.gif"}],"sounds":{"aurora":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/73/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-aurora.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/7/73/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-aurora.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-aurora.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/7/73/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-aurora.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-aurora.wav.ogg"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co